"""English — details, faith (52 questions)."""

D = {
"faith_019": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Ma'idah, in a passage addressed to the People of the Book: \"Indeed, We sent down the Tawrat, in which was guidance and light. By it the prophets who submitted to Allah judged for the Jews, as did the rabbis and the learned\" (5:44). Two verses later the same passage brings 'Isa, son of Maryam, confirming the Tawrat that came before him (5:46), and elsewhere the Qur'an states plainly, \"We gave Musa the Book\" (2:87). A Muslim honours the Tawrat as a true revelation from Allah, given to Musa before the Qur'an.",
"faith_020": "Surah Al-Ma'idah places 'Isa in the line of the prophets who came before him: \"And We sent, following in their footsteps, 'Isa son of Maryam, confirming what came before him of the Tawrat; and We gave him the Injil, in which was guidance and light, confirming what came before it of the Tawrat, and as guidance and admonition for the God-fearing\" (5:46). The next verse tells the people of the Injil to judge by what Allah revealed in it (5:47). Each Book confirms the one before it, and a Muslim believes in the Injil given to 'Isa as one link in that chain of revelation.",
"faith_021": "The verse comes in Surah An-Nisa, right after a verse on interceding for a good cause: \"And when you are greeted with a greeting, greet with one better than it or return it. Indeed, Allah is ever, over all things, an Accountant\" (4:86). The greeting the Qur'an teaches is one of peace: believers entering houses are told to \"greet one another with a greeting from Allah, blessed and good\" (24:61), and the greeting of the people of Paradise is \"Peace\" (10:10). A Muslim answers every salam at least as warmly as it was given, knowing that Allah counts even this small courtesy.",
"faith_022": "Surah Al-A'la closes with a contrast between this life and the next: \"He has succeeded who purifies himself, and remembers the name of his Lord and prays. But you prefer the life of this world, while the Hereafter is better and more lasting\" (87:14-17). Then it says: \"Indeed, this is in the former Scrolls, the Scrolls of Ibrahim and Musa\" (87:18-19). Surah An-Najm also pairs \"the Scrolls of Musa and of Ibrahim, who fulfilled\" what he was charged with (53:36-37). A Muslim learns that the call to purify oneself, pray and prefer the Hereafter was revealed to the prophets long before Muhammad ﷺ.",
"faith_023": "The verse belongs to Surah Az-Zukhruf, where the wrongdoers are described in Hell: \"Indeed, the criminals will be in the punishment of Jahannam, abiding eternally. It will not be lightened for them, and they will despair therein\" (43:74-75). Then comes their cry: \"And they will call, 'O Malik, let your Lord put an end to us!' He will say, 'Indeed, you will remain'\" (43:77). The Qur'an also says that over the Fire are angels, stern and severe, who never disobey what Allah commands them (66:6). A Muslim learns from this scene that the angels carry out Allah's justice exactly, and that the time to seek His mercy is now.",
"faith_024": "Surah Al-Haqqa describes the Day in stages: the Trumpet is blown with a single blast, the earth and the mountains are lifted and crushed with one blow, and the sky splits (69:13-16). Then: \"And the angels are at its edges. And there will bear the Throne of your Lord above them, that Day, eight\" (69:17). The next verse says that on that Day everyone is brought forward and nothing hidden stays hidden (69:18). Surah Ghafir adds that those who carry the Throne glorify their Lord with praise, believe in Him, and ask forgiveness for the believers (40:7). A Muslim learns that the angels nearest the Throne pray for those who believe, and takes comfort in it.",
"faith_025": "Surah As-Sajdah answers those who doubted that they could live again after being lost in the earth (32:10): \"Say: The angel of death, who has been put in charge of you, will take your souls. Then to your Lord you will be returned\" (32:11). The verse that follows shows the wrongdoers on that Day bowing their heads and begging to be sent back to do good (32:12). Elsewhere the Qur'an says that Allah's messengers take the soul \"and they do not fail\" (6:61). A Muslim learns that death is a task carried out exactly at its time, and that what follows it is the return to Allah.",
"faith_026": "The hadith is narrated by 'A'isha and recorded in Sahih Muslim 2996: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said that the angels were created from light, the jinn from a smokeless flame of fire, and Adam from what had been described to us. \"What has been described\" points to the Qur'an, which says that Allah created man from clay (55:14) and the jinn from a smokeless flame of fire (55:15), and that Iblis refused to bow to Adam, saying, \"You created me from fire and created him from clay\" (7:12). A Muslim learns that angels, jinn and humans are three different creations of one Creator, each made from what He chose for it.",
"faith_027": "The verse belongs to Surah Ar-Rahman, which lists the favours of Allah one after another: \"He created man from clay like pottery\" (55:14), \"and He created the jinn from a smokeless flame of fire\" (55:15), followed by the refrain, \"So which of the favours of your Lord will you both deny?\" (55:16). Surah Al-Hijr says the same: the jinn were created before man, from scorching fire (15:27). When Iblis, who was of the jinn (18:50), refused to bow to Adam, he argued from this very origin: \"You created me from fire and created him from clay\" (7:12). A Muslim learns that the jinn are real creatures, unseen but created, and that Allah alone made both them and us.",
"faith_028": "The verse belongs to Surah Fatir, right after a warning not to let this life or \"the Deceiver\" delude us about Allah (35:5): \"Indeed, Satan is an enemy to you; so take him as an enemy. He only invites his party to be among the companions of the Blaze\" (35:6). The Qur'an tells how this enmity began: when Iblis refused to bow to Adam, he vowed to lie in wait for people on the straight path (7:16-17). It also gives the defence: \"When you recite the Qur'an, seek refuge in Allah from Satan, the expelled\" (16:98). A Muslim learns to treat every whisper toward evil as coming from an enemy, and to answer it by seeking refuge in Allah.",
"faith_029": "Surah Al-Ahqaf ends with Allah addressing the Prophet ﷺ: \"So be patient, as the messengers of firm resolve were patient, and do not seek to hasten it for them\" (46:35). The verse does not list their names; Surah Ash-Shura does: Allah ordained for us the religion He enjoined upon Nuh, revealed to Muhammad ﷺ, and enjoined upon Ibrahim, Musa and 'Isa (42:13). Surah Al-Ahzab names the same five when it speaks of the covenant taken from the prophets (33:7). A Muslim learns that these five carried the heaviest missions and held firm, and that the Prophet ﷺ was told to draw on their patience.",
"faith_030": "The verse comes near the end of Surah Az-Zumar, which describes the Day of Resurrection: \"And the Trumpet will be blown, and whoever is in the heavens and whoever is on the earth will fall dead, except whom Allah wills. Then it will be blown again, and at once they will be standing, looking on\" (39:68). The next verse shows the earth shining with the light of its Lord, the record laid open, and the prophets and witnesses brought forward (39:69). Surah Ya-Sin describes the same moment: \"The Trumpet will be blown, and at once from the graves to their Lord they will hasten\" (36:51). A Muslim learns that there are two blasts, one that ends this world and one that raises everyone for judgement.",
"faith_031": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Anbiya: \"And We will set up the scales of justice for the Day of Resurrection, so no soul will be wronged at all. And if there is even the weight of a mustard seed, We will bring it forth. And sufficient are We as accountant\" (21:47). Other surahs tell what the scales decide: \"Those whose scales are heavy, it is they who are successful; and those whose scales are light, it is they who have lost their souls\" (23:102-103). Surah Al-Qari'ah says the same in a few lines (101:6-9). A Muslim learns that no deed is too small to count, and that Allah's weighing is perfectly just.",
"faith_032": "The verse belongs to the closing passage of Surah Al-Kahf. Just before it, the surah describes those whose effort in this life was wasted, whose deeds became worthless and whose recompense is Jahannam (18:103-106). Then comes the contrast: \"Indeed, those who have believed and done righteous deeds, they will have the Gardens of Firdaws as a lodging, abiding therein forever; they will not desire any change from it\" (18:107-108). Surah Al-Mu'minun promises the same reward to the believers who are humble in prayer, turn away from vain talk and give zakat: \"They will inherit al-Firdaws; they will abide therein forever\" (23:10-11). A Muslim learns what earns Firdaws: faith joined to good deeds.",
"faith_033": "The name appears in Surah Al 'Imran, addressed to those who rejected the message: \"Say to those who disbelieve: You will be overcome and gathered to Jahannam, and wretched is the resting place\" (3:12). The Qur'an gives the Fire other names too, each in its own verses: al-Jahim, Saqar (74:26-27), Laza (70:15), al-Hutamah (104:4) and as-Sa'ir, but Jahannam is the one that returns most often. Surah Maryam says that everyone will come to it, and then Allah will save those who feared Him and leave the wrongdoers in it (19:71-72). A Muslim learns these names as warnings meant to keep him on the path, not as a cause for despair.",
"faith_034": "The verse belongs to Surah Al-Qamar, which describes the wrongdoers dragged into the Fire on their faces and told to taste the touch of Saqar (54:47-48). Then comes the statement: \"Indeed, all things We created with measure\" (54:49), and: \"Our command is but one, like the blink of an eye\" (54:50). The surah adds that everything people did is in the records, and every small and great thing is written down (54:52-53). Surah Al-Hadid says the same of events: no disaster strikes the earth or ourselves except that it is in a register before Allah brings it about (57:22). A Muslim learns to meet what happens with patience, knowing it was measured and known by Allah before it came.",
"faith_035": "The verse belongs to Surah An-Nisa: \"Indeed, Allah does not forgive that partners be associated with Him, but He forgives what is less than that for whom He wills. And whoever associates others with Allah has certainly fabricated a tremendous sin\" (4:48). The same surah repeats the warning almost word for word later (4:116), and Luqman gives it to his son: \"O my son, do not associate anything with Allah; indeed, association is a great injustice\" (31:13). The door of repentance stays open in this life: \"Do not despair of the mercy of Allah; indeed, Allah forgives all sins\" (39:53). A Muslim learns to guard the oneness of Allah above everything, and to turn back to Him while there is time.",
"faith_036": "The verse is in Surah Al-Baqarah, at the end of an answer about purity in married life, and it closes with a general truth: \"Indeed, Allah loves those who constantly repent, and He loves those who purify themselves\" (2:222). The Qur'an describes repentance as a change that reaches the deeds: for those who repent, believe and do good, Allah replaces their evil deeds with good ones (25:70), and He calls the believers to \"repent to Allah with sincere repentance\" (66:8). A Muslim learns that a fault followed by sincere return does not push him away from Allah; it is the returning itself that Allah loves.",
"faith_037": "In Surah Nuh, the prophet Nuh tells his Lord how he called his people night and day, openly and in private, and how they only fled further (71:5-9). He recounts his words to them: \"Ask forgiveness of your Lord; indeed, He is ever Forgiving. He will send the sky upon you in abundant rain, and give you increase in wealth and children, and give you gardens and give you rivers\" (71:10-12). The prophet Hud told his people the same: seek forgiveness and repent, and He will send the sky upon you in showers and add strength to your strength (11:52). A Muslim learns that asking forgiveness is not only for the Hereafter; it opens the blessings of this life too.",
"faith_038": "The verse comes in Surah At-Talaq, among rules for families going through divorce, where it lifts the eyes above the hardship: \"And whoever fears Allah, He will make for him a way out, and will provide for him from where he does not expect\" (65:2). \"And whoever relies upon Allah, then He is sufficient for him. Indeed, Allah will accomplish His purpose; Allah has set a measure for everything\" (65:3). When the believers were told that people had gathered against them, they answered, \"Allah is sufficient for us, and He is the best Disposer of affairs\" (3:173). A Muslim learns that reliance follows effort and fear of Allah, and that the outcome then rests safely in His hands.",
"faith_039": "The verse belongs to Surah Ghafir: \"And your Lord says: Call upon Me; I will respond to you. Indeed, those who are too proud to worship Me will enter Jahannam humbled\" (40:60). The verse places supplication inside worship itself, and calls turning away from it pride. Surah Al-Baqarah gives the same promise with great tenderness: \"When My servants ask you about Me, indeed I am near; I respond to the call of the caller when he calls upon Me\" (2:186). A Muslim learns that du'a is never a bother to Allah; He commands it, and He answers it.",
"faith_040": "The verse belongs to Surah Ar-Ra'd, after the disbelievers ask why no sign was sent down and Allah answers that He guides to Himself whoever turns back to Him (13:27). Then He describes those who turn: \"Those who believe and whose hearts find rest in the remembrance of Allah. Unquestionably, by the remembrance of Allah do hearts find rest\" (13:28), and promises them a good state and a good return (13:29). Surah Al-Ahzab tells believers to remember Allah much, morning and evening (33:41-42), and Surah Al-Baqarah says, \"Remember Me; I will remember you\" (2:152). A Muslim learns that peace of heart is not found by chasing it but by remembering Allah.",
"faith_041": "Fajr is the prayer of dawn, prayed after the first light and before sunrise, and the Qur'an names it: it speaks of \"the prayer of Fajr\" and \"the prayer of 'Isha\" in the same verse (24:58), and of \"the recitation of dawn\" that is witnessed (17:78). The same verse orders prayer \"from the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night\" (17:78), the span that holds Dhuhr, 'Asr, Maghrib and 'Isha. Anyone can verify the order by listening to the five calls of a mosque, from Fajr before sunrise to 'Isha at night. A Muslim begins each day with Fajr, before work or school, and so opens the day with Allah.",
"faith_042": "A rak'ah is one unit of the prayer: standing with recitation of the Qur'an, bowing, rising, and two prostrations with a sitting between them. Fajr has two such units, which makes it the shortest of the five, and with Dhuhr, 'Asr and 'Isha at four each and Maghrib at three, the obligatory prayers of a day add up to seventeen rak'ahs. This count is the same in every mosque of the Muslim world, and a child can check it by praying Fajr behind any imam. The Qur'an calls the dawn recitation \"witnessed\" (17:78), and it is recited aloud in both rak'ahs of Fajr. A Muslim learns Fajr first: two rak'ahs that anyone can master.",
"faith_043": "Maghrib is prayed when the sun has set, the first of the night prayers, and the only obligatory prayer with an odd number of rak'ahs: three. Its first two rak'ahs are recited aloud and the third silently, and every Muslim community prays it this way, so a visitor to any mosque at sunset can count it. The Qur'an sets the frame of these times: prayer \"at the two ends of the day and in the early hours of the night\" (11:114), and \"from the decline of the sun until the darkness of the night\" (17:78). In Ramadan the fast is completed \"until the night\" (2:187), and Maghrib marks the moment of breaking it. A Muslim learns Maghrib as the prayer that closes the day's work.",
"faith_044": "The adhan is the public call that tells a whole neighbourhood that a prayer's time has come. It begins with \"Allahu Akbar\" (Allah is the Greatest), carries the two testimonies that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad ﷺ is the Messenger of Allah, then invites, \"Come to prayer, come to success,\" and ends with \"La ilaha illa Allah.\" The Qur'an refers to it: \"When the call to prayer is made on the day of Jumu'ah, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade\" (62:9), and it mentions people who mocked it when the believers called to prayer (5:58). A shorter call, the iqama, is given inside the mosque as the rows form. A Muslim learns to answer the adhan by heading to prayer.",
"faith_045": "The verse of ablution opens a passage of Surah Al-Ma'idah: \"O you who believe, when you rise for prayer, wash your faces and your hands up to the elbows, wipe your heads, and wash your feet up to the ankles\" (5:6). The elbows and the ankles are named so that the limit is clear: the washing reaches them and includes them. The same verse ends by giving the reason: \"Allah does not intend to make difficulty for you, but He intends to purify you and to complete His favour upon you, that you may be grateful\" (5:6). A Muslim learns that wudu is not a chore added to prayer but a purification Allah counts among His favours.",
"faith_046": "Surah Al-Ma'idah gives the allowance in the same verse that teaches ablution: \"But if you are ill or on a journey, or one of you comes from relieving himself, or you have touched women, and you find no water, then seek clean earth and wipe your faces and your hands with it. Allah does not intend to make difficulty for you\" (5:6). Surah An-Nisa gives the same permission in almost the same words (4:43). The ill and the traveller are named first, the two who most often find themselves without water, and the prayer is not dropped for them but made possible. A Muslim learns that Allah makes His worship reachable in every circumstance, in the desert as in illness.",
"faith_047": "The word khutba means an address. On Friday the imam stands on the minbar and delivers it in two parts, sitting briefly between them, and the congregation listens in silence; the Friday prayer of two rak'ahs follows at once. The Qur'an gives the day its weight: \"O you who believe, when the call to prayer is made on the day of Jumu'ah, hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade\" (62:9), and, once the prayer is over, \"disperse in the land and seek of the bounty of Allah\" (62:10). The next verse recalls people who rushed off to a passing trade and \"left you standing\" (62:11), the Prophet ﷺ standing before them. A Muslim learns to arrive before the khutba and to listen to it as part of the worship.",
"faith_048": "Zakat on saved money is a quarter of a tenth (rub' al-'ushr): for every hundred that has stayed above the threshold for a lunar year, two and a half are given. The rate is the same in every book of Islamic law and every zakat calculator, and the arithmetic is simple enough for a child: 2.5 out of every 100. The Qur'an pairs zakat with prayer again and again, as in \"establish prayer and give zakat\" (2:43), and names those who receive it: the poor, the needy, those who collect it, those whose hearts are to be won, captives, debtors, the cause of Allah and the stranded traveller (9:60). It also calls charity a purification of wealth (9:103). A Muslim learns to count his savings each year and set aside Allah's share.",
"faith_049": "The hadith is narrated by Ibn 'Umar in Sahih al-Bukhari 1503: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ made zakat al-fitr obligatory as a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley, on the slave and the free, the male and the female, the young and the old among the Muslims, and he ordered that it be paid before the people go out to the prayer. A sa' is a measure of volume, so the amount is fixed by a quantity of food, not by a sum of money. The Qur'an, at the end of the verses on fasting, tells the believers to complete the count of days and to magnify Allah for having guided them (2:185), and Eid morning opens with that praise. A Muslim gives zakat al-fitr for each member of the household before going out to the Eid prayer.",
"faith_050": "The verse belongs to the passage of Surah Al-Baqarah that lays down the fast: it begins, \"O you who believe, fasting is decreed upon you as it was decreed upon those before you, that you may become God-fearing\" (2:183), and names Ramadan as the month in which the Qur'an was sent down (2:185). Then it sets the day's limits: \"Eat and drink until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct to you from the black thread; then complete the fast until the night\" (2:187). The white thread is the first light spreading along the horizon; the fast runs from that light to sunset. A Muslim learns that the fast has exact edges, and that eating before dawn and breaking at sunset are both part of obeying the verse.",
"faith_051": "The hadith is narrated by Anas ibn Malik and recorded in Sahih al-Bukhari 1923: the Prophet ﷺ said, \"Take the suhur, for indeed there is blessing in the suhur.\" The word suhur comes from sahar, the last part of the night, when this meal is eaten. The Qur'an itself allows eating and drinking \"until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct to you from the black thread\" (2:187), so the meal fits inside the time the verse leaves open. Because the Prophet ﷺ calls it a blessing, the meal is worship as well as food. A Muslim learns to rise before dawn in Ramadan for the suhur, even for a few dates and a drink of water.",
"faith_052": "The word iftar comes from the root that means to break, and the same root gives Eid al-Fitr its name: the festival of breaking the fast. The Qur'an fixes the moment: \"then complete the fast until the night\" (2:187), and night begins when the sun sets, so the fasting person eats as soon as the sun has gone down and then prays Maghrib. Families and mosques often share the iftar together, and in Ramadan it is the meal the whole household gathers around. A Muslim learns that the fast has an appointed end each day, and that breaking it on time is itself obedience, as holding it was.",
"faith_053": "Eid al-Fitr comes on the first day of Shawwal, the morning after the last fast of Ramadan; Eid al-Adha comes on 10 Dhul-Hijja, while the pilgrims are at Mina after standing at 'Arafat. Each begins with a prayer of two rak'ahs in the morning, and each is marked by the takbir, the words \"Allahu Akbar\" repeated aloud. The Qur'an ties both to worship: at the end of the fast believers are to \"complete the number and magnify Allah for having guided you\" (2:185), and in the days of the pilgrimage they are to mention the name of Allah over the animals He provided (22:28). Every Muslim community in the world keeps these two days. A Muslim learns that an Eid is a day of thanks to Allah, opened with prayer before the feast.",
"faith_054": "Surah As-Saffat tells the story. Ibrahim asked for a righteous son and was given good news of a forbearing boy; when the boy could work beside him, Ibrahim told him of a dream in which he sacrificed him, and the son answered, \"O my father, do as you are commanded; you will find me, if Allah wills, of the steadfast\" (37:102). When both had submitted, Allah called, \"O Ibrahim, you have fulfilled the vision\" (37:104-105), and then: \"And We ransomed him with a great sacrifice\" (37:107). The Qur'an adds that neither meat nor blood reaches Allah, only the piety of those who offer it (22:37). A Muslim learns that Eid al-Adha honours a father and a son who put Allah's command before everything.",
"faith_055": "Dhul-Hijja is the twelfth month of the lunar year, and its name means \"the one of the pilgrimage\", because the hajj falls in it every year. The Qur'an says that \"the hajj is in well-known months\" (2:197) and that pilgrimage to the House is a duty owed to Allah by those able to make the journey (3:97); every Muslim calendar marks the days. The rites fill the month's second week: pilgrims go out to Mina on the eighth, stand at 'Arafat on the ninth, keep Eid al-Adha on the tenth, and stone the pillars on the days that follow. A Muslim learns to place the hajj at the very end of the year, after Ramadan and Shawwal have passed.",
"faith_056": "The standing at 'Arafat fills the afternoon of 9 Dhul-Hijja: from midday until sunset the pilgrims stay on the plain outside Makkah, praying and asking forgiveness, and there is no hajj without it. The Qur'an mentions the departure from it: \"And when you pour forth from 'Arafat, remember Allah at the Sacred Monument\" (2:198), the stop at Muzdalifa where the pilgrims spend that night before returning to Mina on the morning of Eid. Every pilgrim stands on the same day, so the date is verified by the hajj itself, year after year. A Muslim learns that the heart of the hajj is not a building but a day spent standing before Allah in prayer.",
"faith_057": "The tawaf is seven circuits, counted from the corner of the Black Stone, with the Ka'ba kept on the pilgrim's left, and it ends where it began; pilgrims then pray two rak'ahs behind the Station of Ibrahim. The Qur'an speaks of those who circle the House: Allah charged Ibrahim and Isma'il to purify His House \"for those who perform tawaf, those who stay for worship, and those who bow and prostrate\" (2:125), and He commands the pilgrims to \"circle the Ancient House\" (22:29). Tawaf is done on arriving, as part of the hajj and the 'umra, and as a farewell before leaving Makkah. A Muslim learns to count the seven quietly, remembering that circling the House is worship of its Lord alone.",
"faith_058": "The sa'y is walked between the two small hills of Safa and Marwa, beside the Sacred Mosque: from Safa to Marwa counts as one lap, back to Safa as the second, and the seventh ends at Marwa. The Qur'an names the two hills: \"Indeed, Safa and Marwa are among the symbols of Allah; so whoever makes hajj to the House or performs 'umra, there is no blame on him for going between them\" (2:158). The place is the valley where Ibrahim settled his family, as he says in his prayer: \"I have settled some of my descendants in an uncultivated valley near Your Sacred House\" (14:37). A Muslim walks the sa'y in the footsteps of Hajar, learning that effort and trust in Allah go together.",
"faith_059": "Ihram begins at a fixed boundary on the way to Makkah, with the intention of hajj or 'umra and the first words of the talbiya; men put on two unsewn white cloths, and women wear their ordinary modest dress. The Qur'an names what the state forbids: \"Whoever undertakes the hajj therein, there is no intimacy, no wrongdoing and no quarrelling during the hajj\" (2:197), and \"do not kill game while you are in ihram\" (5:95). It ends when the rites are done and the pilgrim shaves or cuts the hair. A Muslim learns that ihram strips away rank and fashion: every pilgrim, rich or poor, stands before Allah in the same two cloths.",
"faith_060": "The hadith is narrated by 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar in Sahih al-Bukhari 1549, which records the full talbiya of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: \"Labbayka Allahumma labbayk, labbayka la sharika laka labbayk, inna al-hamda wan-ni'mata laka wal-mulk, la sharika lak\": Here I am, O Allah, here I am; here I am, You have no partner, here I am; indeed all praise, favour and sovereignty are Yours; You have no partner. It is the answer to the call the Qur'an describes: Allah told Ibrahim, \"Proclaim the hajj to the people; they will come to you on foot and on every lean camel, from every distant pass\" (22:27). A Muslim learns the talbiya as the pilgrim's voice: a declaration that he has come, and that Allah has no partner.",
"faith_061": "Three stone pillars stand at Mina: the small, the middle and the great, called Jamrat al-'Aqaba. On 10 Dhul-Hijja, the morning of Eid, pilgrims throw seven pebbles at the great pillar only, saying \"Allahu Akbar\" with each throw; on the days that follow they stone all three, seven pebbles each, and the pebbles are gathered small, the size of a chickpea. The Qur'an speaks of numbered days of remembering Allah, in which leaving after two days or staying longer are both allowed (2:203), and the stoning fills those days at Mina. A Muslim learns that the stoning is a remembrance of Allah, done in order and with the takbir, not a show of anger.",
"faith_062": "The Black Stone is set in the corner of the Ka'ba from which every tawaf starts and at which every circuit is counted; a pilgrim passing it touches or kisses it, or, when the crowd makes that impossible, points toward it and says \"Allahu Akbar\". The Qur'an speaks of the building the Stone belongs to: \"When Ibrahim was raising the foundations of the House, with Isma'il, saying: Our Lord, accept from us\" (2:127), and of Allah's charge to Ibrahim: \"Do not associate anything with Me, and purify My House for those who perform tawaf\" (22:26). A Muslim learns that the Stone marks the start of a worship offered to Allah alone; it is honoured as a sign, never worshipped.",
"faith_063": "The kiswa is the cloth that dresses the Ka'ba: black silk, with a wide belt around its upper part and a curtain over its door, both embroidered in gold thread with verses of the Qur'an. A new kiswa is made each year and the old one is taken down and replaced, and the cloth is seen by every pilgrim who performs tawaf. The house it covers is the one the Qur'an calls \"the Ka'ba, the Sacred House\" that Allah made \"a standing for people\" (5:97), and \"the first House established for mankind\" (3:96). A Muslim learns that the beauty of the kiswa honours the House, and that its sanctity comes from Allah's choice of it, not from any cloth.",
"faith_064": "The 'umra is the shorter visit to the House: the pilgrim enters ihram, performs the tawaf of seven circuits, walks the seven laps of sa'y between Safa and Marwa, and then shaves or cuts the hair; there is no standing at 'Arafat and no stoning at Mina. The Qur'an names both journeys together: \"Complete the hajj and the 'umra for Allah\" (2:196), and it makes the sa'y part of both (2:158). Surah Al-Fath gives the Prophet ﷺ and his Companions the promise of such a visit: \"You will surely enter the Sacred Mosque, if Allah wills, in safety, with heads shaved and hair shortened\" (48:27). A Muslim learns that the 'umra can be made in any month, while the hajj has its fixed days.",
"faith_065": "The name comes in the last verses of Surah Al-Hashr, which lay out the names of Allah one after another: \"He is Allah, other than whom there is no god, the Sovereign, the Pure, the Source of Peace, the Giver of security, the Overseer, the Almighty, the Compeller, the Superior\" (59:23), and then: \"He is Allah, the Creator, the Originator, the Fashioner; to Him belong the best names. Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Him, and He is the Almighty, the Wise\" (59:24). Surah Al-A'raf tells believers what to do with these names: \"To Allah belong the best names, so call upon Him by them\" (7:180). A Muslim learns to use the name Al-Khaliq in praise and in prayer, seeing every creature as His work.",
"faith_066": "The name closes one of the most hopeful verses of the Qur'an, in Surah Az-Zumar: \"Say: O My servants who have transgressed against themselves, do not despair of the mercy of Allah. Indeed, Allah forgives all sins; indeed, He is the Forgiving, the Merciful\" (39:53). The next verse tells what to do with that hope: \"Turn back to your Lord and submit to Him, before the punishment comes upon you and then you are not helped\" (39:54). Allah says elsewhere, \"My mercy encompasses all things\" (7:156). A Muslim learns to say Al-Ghafur not only in praise but as a reason never to give up on himself, and to turn back to Allah while the door is open.",
"faith_067": "The two names close the verse of the Night Journey in Surah Al-Isra: \"Exalted is He who took His servant by night from the Sacred Mosque to the Farthest Mosque, whose surroundings We have blessed, to show him of Our signs. Indeed, He is the Hearing, the Seeing\" (17:1). The same pair appears where Allah describes Himself as unlike anything: \"There is nothing like Him, and He is the Hearing, the Seeing\" (42:11), and when He reassures Musa and Harun before Pharaoh: \"Fear not; indeed, I am with you both, I hear and I see\" (20:46). A Muslim learns that nothing said or done, in the dark of night or the open day, escapes Allah, and that this is a comfort to the one who calls on Him.",
"faith_068": "The name appears in the second verse of Surah Al-Ikhlas, the surah that states the oneness of Allah in four lines: \"Say: He is Allah, the One\" (112:1), \"Allah, as-Samad\" (112:2), \"He neither begets nor is begotten, and there is none equal to Him\" (112:3-4). The verses after the name explain it from both sides: He has no origin and no offspring, and nothing resembles Him, so nothing can be depended on as He is. Surah Fatir says the same in other words: \"O mankind, you are the ones in need of Allah, while Allah is the Free of need, the Praiseworthy\" (35:15). A Muslim learns to bring every need to As-Samad, because He alone needs nothing and is needed by all.",
"faith_069": "The Throne Verse in Surah Al-Baqarah opens with these names: \"Allah, there is no god but Him, the Ever-Living, the Self-Sustaining. Neither drowsiness overtakes Him nor sleep\" (2:255). The verse goes on: to Him belongs all that is in the heavens and the earth, none intercedes with Him except by His permission, He knows what is before and behind His creatures, His Kursi extends over the heavens and the earth, and guarding them does not tire Him (2:255). The same two names open Surah Al 'Imran (3:2), and Allah says, \"Rely upon the Ever-Living who does not die\" (25:58). A Muslim learns that the One he relies on never sleeps, never dies and never tires of watching over him.",
"faith_070": "The verse belongs to Surah Adh-Dhariyat, right after Allah states why He created us: \"I did not create the jinn and mankind except to worship Me. I do not want from them any provision, nor do I want them to feed Me\" (51:56-57). Then: \"Indeed, it is Allah who is the Provider, the Possessor of strength, the Firm\" (51:58). The name answers a fear: the one who gives his time to worship will not go without, because provision comes from Allah, not from the worshipper's worry. Surah Hud says that there is no creature on the earth but its provision is upon Allah (11:6), and Surah Al-'Ankabut that many a creature carries no provision, yet Allah provides for it and for us (29:60). A Muslim learns to seek his livelihood with effort and without anxiety, trusting Ar-Razzaq.",
}
